  "summary": "No prior work was found that directly addresses the specific question of demonstrating a design's ability to detect personnel effects on shot mix by observing changes in the offensive five. While general research on lineup effects and shot selection exists, this question is framed as a methodological validation."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "Changes in the offensive five (substitutions) are not random. Coaches make substitutions for strategic reasons, in response to game state, player performance, fatigue, or opponent matchups. These confounding factors can also influence shot mix, making it difficult to isolate the causal effect of the personnel change itself."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "While there are many substitutions, the number of possessions for specific 'before' and 'after' lineup configurations, especially for distinct player combinations, might be limited, potentially reducing the power to detect subtle but real changes in shot mix."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "The definition of 'shot mix' needs to be precise (e.g., proportion of 3-pointers, mid-range shots, shots at the rim). The stability and sensitivity of these measures over short intervals (e.g., a few possessions after a substitution) could affect the reliability of detecting changes."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.5,
  "rejected": 0.1,
  "untestable": 0.05,
  "artifact": 0.25,
  "uncertain": 0.1
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The causal effect of a change in the 'offensive five' (lineup) on the team's subsequent shot mix, specifically quantified as the difference in the distribution of shot attempts (e.g., proportion of 3-pointers, shots at the rim, long mid-range) in the possessions immediately following the lineup change compared to the possessions immediately preceding it.",
  "unit": "A 'lineup interval' defined as a sequence of consecutive possessions played by the same five players for a given team. The change event is a substitution that alters the offensive five.",
  "sample": "All substitutions across all teams in NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, and 2024-25 regular seasons and playoffs where at least one player on the court for a team changes. Focus on intervals with a minimum of 5 possessions before and after the substitution to ensure sufficient data for shot mix calculation.",
  "power": "The study aims to detect a statistically significant change in at least one key shot mix metric (e.g., a 2 percentage point shift in 3-point attempt rate) with 80% power at an alpha level of 0.05. A sufficiently large number of substitutions is expected to provide adequate power for detecting such changes, especially after pooling data across seasons and teams.",
  "controls": "To mitigate confounding, a difference-in-differences approach or a regression model will be used. Controls will include: game state (score differential, time remaining in quarter/game), opponent identity, home/away status, the specific players entering and exiting the game, and potentially team-level offensive efficiency and pace prior to the substitution. Matching on game context and opponent identity will also be considered.",
  "falsification": "A falsification test will involve comparing the observed changes in shot mix for actual offensive five changes against 'pseudo-changes' where no substitution occurred, or where a substitution occurred but did not change the offensive five (e.g., a defensive player entering for another defensive player, which is less common in NBA). If the observed effects are primarily due to personnel changes, these pseudo-changes should not exhibit similar patterns.",
  "multiplicity": "Multiple shot mix metrics will be examined (e.g., proportion of shots from 0-3ft, 3-10ft, 10-16ft, 16ft-3P line, 3P). A Bonferroni correction or a False Discovery Rate (FDR) control method will be applied to adjust for multiple comparisons.",
  "decision_rule": "The design will be considered successful in demonstrating its ability to detect personnel effects if, after controlling for confounding factors, a statistically significant change (p < 0.05, adjusted for multiplicity) is observed in at least one primary shot mix metric following a change in the offensive five, and this effect is not observed in falsification tests.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2023-24 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2024-25 regular season and playoffs."
 }
}

